Read inputs/Q.tex and inputs/P.tex. The scan that selected this pair judged
it from the abstracts alone, feasibility 40 and gain 55
out of 100, and named this connexion: Replace Q's expected-value stochastic satisfaction functions with CVaR-based risk-averse objectives and use P's zeroth-order CVaR gradient estimator inside Q's VS-PBR best-response loop, then check whether Q's LMI-derived quadratic payment mechanism still implements the social-welfare NE with budget balance and individual rationality, and whether mean-square convergence survives CVaR's non-smoothness and estimator bias. Its reasoning:
Both pieces are concretely combinable (Q needs a sampled-feedback best-response subroutine, P supplies exactly that for CVaR objectives), but reworking Q's stability/uniqueness proofs and mechanism guarantees under a non-smooth risk measure is substantial theoretical work, not a quick experiment, so feasibility is moderate. The gain is more than incremental because it targets a specific technical incompatibility (LMI-based quadratic payment stability versus CVaR's kink and finite-sample bias) rather than a generic pairing, though risk-averse mechanism design is an existing subfield so it stops short of decisive. Treat that as a first hypothesis, not an instruction; confirm
it, sharpen it or replace it. Then look for an advance that neither
paper makes alone: a result one supplies that the other can use, a claim
one makes that the other tests or breaks, a construction that combines
them, a question that only becomes visible with both in view. Prefer one
concrete, supported line over several vague ones. Sharpen the question if
that helps, and say when you have changed it. No quota of ideas and no
forced positive result; "nothing here" with reasons is a valid finding.

